{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE pointer with per-key layering\n\n## Context / Problem\nCI and a local developer need different LLM configuration for the same checkout. Today the only\nconfig sources are discovered by walking up from the repo root, so an environment cannot point rebar\nat its own file without editing the repo's.\n\n## Approach\nAdd `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE`, naming a TOML file whose keys LAYER over the discovered config rather\nthan replacing it. Layering is deliberate: an environment overriding one key must not restate the\nwhole config, because that duplication is a likelier source of drift than precedence subtlety.\nPrecedence becomes: CLI `-c llm.KEY` > `REBAR_LLM_<KEY>` env > REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE > discovered\nconfig file > default.\n\nPrior art for the pointer itself: `KUBECONFIG`, `AWS_CONFIG_FILE`, `DOCKER_CONFIG` all use an env var\nnaming a config file. Those REPLACE; rebar layers, for the duplication reason above.\n\nSecondary benefit: hermetic tests. Pointing at an empty file makes model resolution independent of\nwhatever `rebar.toml` is up-tree — the same trick `AWS_CONFIG_FILE=/dev/null` provides for boto3.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` and/or `src/rebar/_config_sources.py` — the pointer and its layering.\n- `tests/unit/` — precedence tests.\n\n## Testing\nUnit tier: config resolution only.\n- a key present ONLY in the pointed file is used.\n- a key present in BOTH is taken from the pointed file.\n- a key present ONLY in the discovered file survives (this is what distinguishes layering from replace).\n- env still beats the pointed file.\n- a pointer naming a nonexistent file fails loudly rather than silently falling back.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] a test asserts a key set only in the discovered config survives when the pointer supplies other keys\n- [ ] a test asserts the pointed file wins per-key over the discovered config\n- [ ] a test asserts `REBAR_LLM_<KEY>` env still beats the pointed file\n- [ ] a test asserts a pointer to a missing path raises a typed rebar config error naming the variable\n- [ ] `docs/env-vars.md` lists `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE` after regeneration\n- [ ] a test asserts NESTED tables DEEP-merge: a pointed file setting only `frontier.model`, over a\n      discovered config carrying `frontier.provider` AND a `frontier.fallback` chain, yields the\n      overridden model AND the discovered provider AND the discovered fallback chain. This is the\n      criterion that fails against `dict.update` semantics — the existing shallow merge drops both\n      siblings, so the test is not satisfiable by the current machinery and cannot pass vacuously.\n- [ ] a test asserts ARRAYS REPLACE rather than append: a `fallback` chain in the pointed file yields\n      EXACTLY that chain (not the concatenation with the discovered one), and `fallback = []` yields\n      no fallbacks at all — the \"CI pins fail-loudly over a project default\" case.\n- [ ] a test asserts `read_reserved_section`'s own SHALLOW-merge behaviour is UNCHANGED for a non-llm\n      reserved section, proving the deep merge did not leak into shared config machinery. The parent\n      plan forbids editing that helper; this criterion is what detects the mis-scope.\n\n## Merge semantics (decided on the parent, eb58 — do not re-derive)\n\nDeep/recursive over tables (leaf key wins); arrays replaced wholesale, never appended. Precedence:\nCLI `-c` > env > `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE` > discovered file > default. Secrets stay env-only.\n\n**Do NOT change `read_reserved_section`** (`config.py:588-600`). Its `merged.update(sub)` is shared by\nevery reserved section; deepening it would silently alter layering for unrelated consumers. Implement\nthe deep merge in the llm resolver over the section dict it receives. Editing that helper means the\nchange is mis-scoped."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785479978733219001,"uuid":"30b4a2a1-9571-40e5-9c6f-d53cac4278bb"}